(369395, 29026, 6999, '<td valign="top">Critique Club time for you ... <br/> <br/>I guess there''s a certain creepiness to this image - suggestions of mines, and of dark places underground, and of surfacing. The nebulous quality of the presentation suggests dreams, or rather nightmares, with thier attendant element of hope, of possible salvation. There are elements that speak to me of rottenness - like peering into a bottle of soured milk in a desperate 3a.m. hunt for something in the fridge; or like scrabbling in the gutter in a confused drunken haze, unable to focus, wondering blearily if that light is a car, or a train, or might just perhaps be the bedside lamp and actually one is safe at home in bed.<br/> <br/>If it was eant to be a figurative image ... I''m impressed that someone was able to work out what it is - I couldn''t. I really don''t know what else to say - I''m not about to start listing the technical faults with the image: they''re legion, and surely very obvious.<br/> <br/>ed</td>', 'Critique Club time for you ...  I guess there''s a certain creepiness to this image - suggestions of mines, and of dark places underground, and of surfacing. The nebulous quality of the presentation suggests dreams, or rather nightmares, with thier attendant element of hope, of possible salvation. There are elements that speak to me of rottenness - like peering into a bottle of soured milk in a desperate 3a.m. hunt for something in the fridge; or like scrabbling in the gutter in a confused drunken haze, unable to focus, wondering blearily if that light is a car, or a train, or might just perhaps be the bedside lamp and actually one is safe at home in bed. If it was eant to be a figurative image ... I''m impressed that someone was able to work out what it is - I couldn''t. I really don''t know what else to say - I''m not about to start listing the technical faults with the image: they''re legion, and surely very obvious. ed', '2003-07-29 08:25:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(369408, 28940, 6999, '<td valign="top">Greetings from Critique Club Asit.<br/> <br/>Lots of problems with this shot I''m afraid, though I like tthe fact that you didn''t take a full moon shot (although your comments suggest that you just shot when you could, rather thna choosing this moment). I thinka  partial moon gives a much stronger impression of three-dimensional roundness than a full moon, which appears so flat in photographs.<br/> <br/>You''ve obviously taken the re-sizing way too far - look especially along the edge of the shadow line where the pixelation is absolutely awful. At this size, there ought to be a lot more visible detail in the craters and ridges, and there is harly anything visible here. It''s also made the edge of the moon appear out of focus. I''m quite surprised with a 5mp camera with that zoom on it that you couldn''t get closer to it.<br/> <br/>I''m not a fan of commenting on borders - this is a photo site, not a design site - but you chose that colour for it and added it, and I must say that I can see no good reason for it: the colour doesn''t connect with the image at all, and in fact draws the eye away from the picture.<br/> <br/>Was intrigued also to read your comments on the other moon photos.<br/> <br/>Ed</td>', 'Greetings from Critique Club Asit. Lots of problems with this shot I''m afraid, though I like tthe fact that you didn''t take a full moon shot (although your comments suggest that you just shot when you could, rather thna choosing this moment). I thinka  partial moon gives a much stronger impression of three-dimensional roundness than a full moon, which appears so flat in photographs. You''ve obviously taken the re-sizing way too far - look especially along the edge of the shadow line where the pixelation is absolutely awful. At this size, there ought to be a lot more visible detail in the craters and ridges, and there is harly anything visible here. It''s also made the edge of the moon appear out of focus. I''m quite surprised with a 5mp camera with that zoom on it that you couldn''t get closer to it. I''m not a fan of commenting on borders - this is a photo site, not a design site - but you chose that colour for it and added it, and I must say that I can see no good reason for it: the colour doesn''t connect with the image at all, and in fact draws the eye away from the picture. Was intrigued also to read your comments on the other moon photos. Ed', '2003-07-29 08:41:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(369410, 29135, 6999, '<td valign="top">Good solid technical work - though perhaps lacking in any real inspiration as far as the challenge is concerned. I think lighting is a bit of an issue - it''s very flat and very frontal, and that takes away a lot of the three-dimensionality of the subject - almost as though you were afraid to get any shadows in the shot.</td>', 'Good solid technical work - though perhaps lacking in any real inspiration as far as the challenge is concerned. I think lighting is a bit of an issue - it''s very flat and very frontal, and that takes away a lot of the three-dimensionality of the subject - almost as though you were afraid to get any shadows in the shot.', '2003-07-29 08:43:24.000000', NULL, 0),
